00:11:28,770 --> 00:11:33,220
some ideas about follow-on
work or next steps.

280
00:11:35,060 --> 00:11:41,205
One other part for the on-campus
version of this course,

281
00:11:41,205 --> 00:11:43,890
we'll have a final project
Scrum that we do weekly

282
00:11:43,890 --> 00:11:46,600
with all of the people

283
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:48,445
who are taking the
course synchronously.

284
00:11:48,445 --> 00:11:49,480
For those of you who are

285
00:11:49,480 --> 00:11:51,190
taking this course
asynchronously,

286
00:11:51,190 --> 00:11:53,020
you may think about scheduling

287
00:11:53,020 --> 00:11:55,600
one of these with
your teammates.

288
00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:58,180
The main idea behind the Scrum,

289
00:11:58,180 --> 00:11:59,200
it's the same thing
that we talked

290
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:00,775
about with the Agile content.

291
00:12:00,775 --> 00:12:02,515
You want to be able to
answer the questions,

292
00:12:02,515 --> 00:12:04,480
what did you do since
the last report?

293
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:06,790
What are you going to do
before the next report?

294
00:12:06,790 --> 00:12:08,485
Are there any blockers?

295
00:12:08,485 --> 00:12:11,485
Listen, especially, for
the blockers thing.

296
00:12:11,485 --> 00:12:13,629
As blockers are identified,

297
00:12:13,629 --> 00:12:15,160
think about how you
can work together

298
00:12:15,160 --> 00:12:16,900
as a team to help resolve them.

299
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:19,030
Or possibly how you
could leverage help from

300
00:12:19,030 --> 00:12:22,135
other teams to resolve
any blocking issues.

301
00:12:22,135 --> 00:12:24,130
Another strategy you
could possibly use

302
00:12:24,130 --> 00:12:25,930
with blockers is to

303
00:12:25,930 --> 00:12:27,880
reorganize your work such

304
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:30,285
that you can complete
something else.

305
00:12:30,285 --> 00:12:31,780
For instance, if
you're waiting for

306
00:12:31,780 --> 00:12:33,095
delivery or something,

307
00:12:33,095 --> 00:12:34,840
maybe think about how
you could reorganize

308
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:36,325
the work such that you could

309
00:12:36,325 --> 00:12:37,765
avoid being blocked by

310
00:12:37,765 --> 00:12:40,465
an issue that's
outside your control.

311
00:12:40,465 --> 00:12:43,620
A bit more about
final project ideas.

312
00:12:43,620 --> 00:12:45,535
If you're stuck or
have trouble thinking

313
00:12:45,535 --> 00:12:47,730
about what you want to do
for the final project.

314
00:12:47,730 --> 00:12:49,740
You might want to consider
something we haven't covered

315
00:12:49,740 --> 00:12:52,020
fully in class or assignments.

316
00:12:52,020 --> 00:12:53,950
Look at some content we may have

317
00:12:53,950 --> 00:12:55,870
partially covered that
looked interesting to you.

318
00:12:55,870 --> 00:12:56,995
Try to build onto that,

319
00:12:56,995 --> 00:12:59,110
explore that content
a little more.

320
00:12:59,110 --> 00:13:01,390
Look for projects which
have done similar things

321
00:13:01,390 --> 00:13:04,315
online to something that
sounds interesting.

322
00:13:04,315 --> 00:13:06,995
Read blogs about
things that people

323
00:13:06,995 --> 00:13:08,105
have done and maybe try to

324
00:13:08,105 --> 00:13:09,830
build on some existing projects.

325
00:13:09,830 --> 00:13:12,235
In some cases,
projects might not

326
00:13:12,235 --> 00:13:15,080
be built with something
like Buildroot or Yocto.

327
00:13:15,080 --> 00:13:19,555
You might want to put a
standalone demo project

328
00:13:19,555 --> 00:13:21,220
into something that
you can build as

329
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:23,870
a deployable image with
Buildroot or Yocto.

330
00:13:23,870 --> 00:13:25,420
Or maybe something's
been done on

331
00:13:25,420 --> 00:13:27,310
a different hardware
platform and you want

332
00:13:27,310 --> 00:13:29,080
to create an image
for it that would

333
00:13:29,080 --> 00:13:31,600
run on Raspberry
Pi for instance.

334
00:13:31,600 --> 00:13:33,370
Those are some ideas
about what you

335
00:13:33,370 --> 00:13:35,105
could do for a final project.

336
00:13:35,105 --> 00:13:37,840
One thing I caution
everybody on is,

337
00:13:37,840 --> 00:13:40,650
although the agile
scrum methodology lets

338
00:13:40,650 --> 00:13:42,820
you change your plan

339
00:13:42,820 --> 00:13:45,040
in terms of how you plan
to implement things.

340
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:47,530
One thing I've seen
students struggle with in

341
00:13:47,530 --> 00:13:50,230
the past is if you
start the project

342
00:13:50,230 --> 00:13:52,475
without really
having any plan for

343
00:13:52,475 --> 00:13:54,245
implementing some of the more

344
00:13:54,245 --> 00:13:56,275
major of the project components,

345
00:13:56,275 --> 00:13:58,835
it may become very
difficult by the end

346
00:13:58,835 --> 00:14:02,065
to have anything to
actually submit.

347
00:14:02,065 --> 00:14:04,510
Think about having
a plan for any

348
00:14:04,510 --> 00:14:06,380
of the major project
components you're going to do.

349
00:14:06,380 --> 00:14:07,660
Don't assume that it's just

350
00:14:07,660 --> 00:14:09,775
going to all come
together at the end.

351
00:14:09,775 --> 00:14:11,800
Think about how
you can structure

352
00:14:11,800 --> 00:14:15,305
your project sprints
such that you're

353
00:14:15,305 --> 00:14:17,400
doing some work at the
beginning to make sure

354
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:18,485
the most critical parts of

355
00:14:18,485 --> 00:14:21,425
your project are
proven out early.

356
00:14:21,425 --> 00:14:22,840
There are a lot of

357
00:14:22,840 --> 00:14:24,730
different project
components that you can

358
00:14:24,730 --> 00:14:26,650
leverage that are linked

359
00:14:26,650 --> 00:14:28,750
from our supported
hardware platform site.

360
00:14:28,750 --> 00:14:32,090
The wiki pages are also
useful to search for this.

361
00:14:32,090 --> 00:14:34,540
Students from previous
semesters have done work

362
00:14:34,540 --> 00:14:37,685
to giving you common
project components like

