00:14:37,685 --> 00:14:41,495
UART or things that you
might find useful for

364
00:14:41,495 --> 00:14:45,745
your implementations and for
specific hardware platforms.

365
00:14:45,745 --> 00:14:47,290
So take a look at those.

366
00:14:47,290 --> 00:14:48,670
Also as I mentioned before,

367
00:14:48,670 --> 00:14:50,650
look at the past project section

368
00:14:50,650 --> 00:14:53,750
of the on-campus course website.

369
00:14:53,880 --> 00:14:59,290
In terms of how to retarget
for Buildroot for hardware,

370
00:14:59,290 --> 00:15:02,915
there's a section on this
that goes over the details.

371
00:15:02,915 --> 00:15:04,340
But it's really about,

372
00:15:04,340 --> 00:15:07,090
there's a shared
script that exists

373
00:15:07,090 --> 00:15:10,045
in the Buildroot
assignment base repo,

374
00:15:10,045 --> 00:15:13,070
that you can use to select
the hardware configuration.

375
00:15:13,070 --> 00:15:15,385
Once you change the
hardware configuration,

376
00:15:15,385 --> 00:15:16,810
you want to clean so that you

377
00:15:16,810 --> 00:15:18,400
start with a new build targeting

378
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:20,315
that specific configuration and

379
00:15:20,315 --> 00:15:22,475
then rerun the build dot sh.

380
00:15:22,475 --> 00:15:25,810
That's going to use the new
configuration setup for

381
00:15:25,810 --> 00:15:30,250
your hardware based on
this shared dot sh script.

382
00:15:30,250 --> 00:15:32,735
Then do the same
save config that we

383
00:15:32,735 --> 00:15:34,880
did previously with the
Buildroot assignments.

384
00:15:34,880 --> 00:15:37,690
That's going to save that
modified config file

385
00:15:37,690 --> 00:15:40,330
to your repo and
allow your teammates,

386
00:15:40,330 --> 00:15:41,830
for instance, to build with

387
00:15:41,830 --> 00:15:44,360
the same configuration
you're using.

388
00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:50,184
Once you switch to use
a different project,

389
00:15:50,184 --> 00:15:53,475
you'll also start with a
brand new configuration file.

390
00:15:53,475 --> 00:15:56,400
You'll need to use Buildroot
and menu config to add

391
00:15:56,400 --> 00:15:59,650
any packages that you
want back into the build.

392
00:15:59,650 --> 00:16:01,225
For instance, Dropbear,

393
00:16:01,225 --> 00:16:03,270
changing the root password,

394
00:16:03,270 --> 00:16:05,590
adding the assignment
packages as

395
00:16:05,590 --> 00:16:07,210
necessary if you plan to

396
00:16:07,210 --> 00:16:09,550
leverage some of your
assignment content.

397
00:16:09,550 --> 00:16:11,315
Then setting up the
root file system

398
00:16:11,315 --> 00:16:13,730
overlay the same steps that
we've done previously.

399
00:16:13,730 --> 00:16:15,400
You'll need to redo once you

400
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:18,050
retarget for a different
hardware platform.

401
00:16:18,050 --> 00:16:22,085
Then save your configuration
file and rebuild to start

402
00:16:22,085 --> 00:16:23,950
the configuration or start

403
00:16:23,950 --> 00:16:26,890
the build for this
target platform.

404
00:16:27,120 --> 00:16:30,850
One thing also to know
as we move away from

405
00:16:30,850 --> 00:16:35,000
QEMU and into a hardware-based
implementation,

406
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,140
you'll find that
reflashing the image

407
00:16:37,140 --> 00:16:39,985
for every change is
quite time-consuming.

408
00:16:39,985 --> 00:16:42,100
There's a couple of
different approaches you can

409
00:16:42,100 --> 00:16:43,735
use for avoiding this.

410
00:16:43,735 --> 00:16:45,565
One is depending
on your project,

411
00:16:45,565 --> 00:16:48,280
you might be able to still
leverage the QEMU build.

412
00:16:48,280 --> 00:16:51,790
You may be able to still test
your application in QEMU

413
00:16:51,790 --> 00:16:53,020
and get it working that

414
00:16:53,020 --> 00:16:55,810
way and it might be
quicker to iterate there.

415
00:16:55,810 --> 00:17:00,010
Another successful strategy
is to copy executables from

416
00:17:00,010 --> 00:17:01,900
your output directory
directly to

417
00:17:01,900 --> 00:17:04,784
the file system using SCP.

418
00:17:04,784 --> 00:17:08,465
We've covered SCP in
previous courses as well,

419
00:17:08,465 --> 00:17:09,515
but it's basically just a way to

420
00:17:09,515 --> 00:17:14,330
transfer files to and
from an SSH target.

421
00:17:14,330 --> 00:17:17,285
You can just take the
output of your build

422
00:17:17,285 --> 00:17:20,910
and target files and SCP them.

423
00:17:20,910 --> 00:17:22,435
If you know which
files are changing,

424
00:17:22,435 --> 00:17:25,160
just copy them directly
over to the target.

425
00:17:25,160 --> 00:17:28,030
Then once you get everything
working, of course,

426
00:17:28,030 --> 00:17:30,005
you can make the
changes and rebuild

427
00:17:30,005 --> 00:17:33,125
the entire image and retest
it all as one component.

428
00:17:33,125 --> 00:17:35,110
That's pretty much the basics of

429
00:17:35,110 --> 00:17:37,470
the final project overview.

430
00:17:37,470 --> 00:17:40,840
As I mentioned, I tried to
keep this very generic.

431
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:42,785
The idea is that you
can cover whatever

432
00:17:42,785 --> 00:17:45,505
you find interesting
for your final project.

433
00:17:45,505 --> 00:17:49,370
If you happen to be
working on a work project,

434
00:17:49,370 --> 00:17:51,605
you might want to think
about how you could do

435
00:17:51,605 --> 00:17:55,330
some portion of a
project investigation

436
00:17:55,330 --> 00:17:57,545
that could be useful
for your work as well.

437
00:17:57,545 --> 00:17:59,350
Really anything that you find

438
00:17:59,350 --> 00:18:01,120
interesting that can align with

439
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:04,605
the tasks that we talked
about in this course,

